From: Catalyst Linux
Subject: [PATCH] panel-mipi-dbi-m: flushes clear of the scan at the link's full speed

Patch 0003 timed each flush from the tearing-effect line for a link slower
than the panel's scan (54 to 65 us a row against 49), and led the scan from
above the band otherwise. Patch 0007 then took the link to 62.5 MHz: about
44 us a row, faster than the scan. A whole frame then always led: it waited
for the next blank (8 ms on average, almost a refresh at worst), then spent
its 10 rows of porch, and more, on the swap of every pixel's bytes, so the
write set off behind the scan, caught it up 60 or so rows down, and tore
there, every frame.

- One rule, whichever is faster: the band's first row lands a time d after
  the scan has passed it, with d such that every row of the band is
  written after this refresh's scan has read it and before the next one's
  does. Over the band d changes by rows x (write's row time - scan's), so
  the window is most of a period: at 62.5 MHz and 59.9 Hz, all but about
  3.3 ms of 16.7. Most flushes go at once; the rest wait for the window's
  start (worked through on 20,000 random flushes: none torn, one in six
  waited, the longest 3.4 ms; patch 0003's timing tore all of them).
- The pixels' bytes are swapped, and the window set, before the wait, so
  that the wait ends where the first pixel goes out and the time measured
  per pixel is the wire's alone.
- A larger margin ahead of the next refresh's scan (1 ms) than behind this
  one's (0.3 ms): a write held up on its way can only fall back towards
  the next.
- The edge's time as best known: an interrupt is only ever late, so an
  edge sooner than due is taken as it is and a later one only eases the
  timing on.
- A wake-up late past its window waits again (three tries).
- Before the first flush is measured, a row's time comes from link_hz.
- tearing_effect counts at_once, waited and unsynced flushes (the shell
  reads only state and period_ns).

The panel's rate is set by its init sequence: 59.9 Hz in the portrait one
(FRCTRL2 0x10, scripts/panel-portrait-blob.py). Without `te-gpios` nothing
changes.

